such web apps get LOTS of people signing up from Beverly Hills
because people overseas know the zip code &quot;90210&quot; ;)
ah--that's funny!
you force people to fill in form fields they don't want to, and they'll just put in crap. and no amount of validation code you write can detect it.
required form fields = futile.
bad UI
same thing with required properties for web formats.
basically it's elementary UI/UX/psych
understood--and I agree
but format designers (especially developers) rarely think about such things :)
consider this
(probably worth a wiki page or article)
&quot;SHOULD&quot; is perfectly reasonable though
as you get more specific, or perhaps, specialized
i.e. from hatom -&gt; news
Atom is a prime example of this problem actually
and even hAtom too
the idea of adding more optional fields in my mind just means fluff
we're learning lessons
in other words, the reason for introducing a subclass of a microformat
should be to introduce real value
you don't get more value through fiat
you get more value through incentive
true
but there is no fiat that says you have to subclass from hatom to news
if you don't have the added fields, stick with hatom
one of the key benefits I've been thinking about with news is that parsers already understand most of it
they understand the hatom part
right - which might make a good case for adding the new optional fields directly to an iteration to hAtom! (e.g. perhaps hAtom 0.3)
(depending on research and uptake)
(another brainstorm option to consider)
thinking
(good to explore separately first - more modular)
agreed
I would think that adding in more and more fields that aren't directly associated with Atom
would &quot;tear&quot; at the intentions of hAtom
and that subclassing (or inheriting) is a cleaner option for parsers and publishers alike
in other words--and in our case--if you're a news publisher who doesn't want to use the news format--at least use hatom (and then you're most of the way there)
or thinking of news as a news mixin
even a rel-principles could stand alone as its own mixin
right
and should, I think
I can't speak for the Media Standards Trust team
but from what I've seen, they have a big roadmap for journalistic principles
a subclass (or mixin) of rel-principles
&quot;roadmap&quot; - that's one of those enterprise words right ? ;)
hah
I agree that adding in non-Atom fields to hAtom could cloud the meaning/clarity of hAtom
enterprise indeed--a nice fluffy word for &quot;something I haven't been briefed on yet :)
yes--one of the issues we had from the start was the use of &quot;source&quot;
Having worked at more than my fair share of BigCos, I've seen far too many &quot;roadmaps&quot; that have nothing to do with the actual territory
critical for news, implied in url everywhere else, I think
lol, true enough
&quot;source&quot; maybe another way of stating the citation problem
may be
well, that's an interesting take
that's the third definition of source we find in news
do you know any news folks that use the &lt;cite&gt; tag in their HTML today?
hmmm--let me check with Stuart
I know that as we ramp up our own systems for hnews
things like that will become easier
