CONVERSATION:
lsmith: sleeping bag in the office... thats the spirit! :)
ap ap ap, &quot;Rebuild if 5% of the rows have changed&quot;... not true for oracle ...
why do people love to rebuild indexes..
hali :)
there is a very asktom thread about index rebuilding and someone referes to an article by donald burelson ... tom responds with &quot;yes, very pretty graphs indeed. he likes graphs, but not facts&quot;
unless you do really really massive dml you almost never need to rebuild an index..
the index becomes better and faster unless something redical happens... oracle collects more and more stats about it
hali: similar advice can be found in the oracle sql tuning pocket reference by oreilly
although the author refrains from giving an exact percentage
burleson claims to be an expert... tom responds
I'm sure DB is an &quot;expert&quot; based on the definition:
- experts CLAIM things
- scientists PROVE things
:)
*smack*
found the thread
<a href="http://asktom.oracle.com/pls/ask/f?p=4950">http://asktom.oracle.com/pls/ask/f?p=4950</a>:8:3521033655157586064::NO::F4950_P8_DISPLAYID,F4950_P8_CRITERIA:2913600659112
worth a quick browse
spitzenklasse
anyways .. if you are talking about a table where you delete as many rows as you insert
then it will probably all run even in the long run
however if you have a table that has very different amount of data in different situations it can be tricky
indeed
then partitioning could be fun
then again .. the question if you are then really hurting from getting the same speed when the table is close to empty
as you did when it was full
speaking of that...
