conv_id=57234 | prompt="
CONVERSATION:
Atamido, no, the overcorrection was the changing of proper nouns (names of places etc.) which should use their native spelling.
a11aabeeceeae6b8cb5d12ea06b56554 that difference between ..." | raw="
CONVERSATION:
Atamido, no, the overcorrection was the changing of proper nouns (names of places etc.) which should use their native spelling.
a11aabeeceeae6b8cb5d12ea06b56554 that difference between hCalendar and iCalendar is handled by converters.  hCalendar is more author friendly than iCalendar in a manner that is trivially deterministically converted if necessary.
a11aabeeceeae6b8cb5d12ea06b56554, feel free to note any problems you find with hResume on the /wiki/hresume-issues page
tantek: The difference is undocumented though, it just says that hCal is just a serialization of iCal, and then silently changes it
where does it say that hCal is just a serialization of iCal ?
tantek: I'd say it was implied by &quot;The basic format of hCalendar is to use iCalendar object/property names in lower-case for class names, and to map the nesting of iCalendar objects directly into nested XHTML elements.&quot;
a11aabeeceeae6b8cb5d12ea06b56554, it's not like whitespace handling is the same either
it's kinda silly to assume that syntax details like that are identical across two different context formats
so no, I disagree that it is implied
Both are defined, in this case, to be ISO 8601 dates, just in iCal's case a subset thereof. Allowing all forms in hCal seems silly
Then parsers have to cope with all three forms of date
(Instead of the one allowed in iCal.)
authors are more important that parsers
disallowing it in iCal was silly
How many authors want to use week or ordinal dates?
many authors use whole dates without times
that was the use case that drove that decision
whereas iCal requires fully specifying the datetime to seconds
which is silly, because authors don't do that
a11aabeeceeae6b8cb5d12ea06b56554, I'm very surprised to hear you arguing from the &quot;optimize for parsers/parsing&quot; viewpoint, as that's a common trap
that leads to bad (author unfriendly) design
I'm not per-se arguing that, I'm just wemtioning that as a side effect
Calendar dates are fine. I see no reason to allow week/ordinal dates.
I'd be tempted to somewhat ruthlessly apply the 80/20 rule here, and disallow week/ordinal dates, which allow nothing that can't be given as a calendar date
introducing limitations like that makes it harder for authors to keep track, whereas &quot;ISO8601 dates are valid&quot; is easier to remember.
How about RFC 3339?
the benefit from excluding that portion is less than the cost of doing so
IIRC RFC3339 also requires seconds to be specified on datetimes, which is also silly, given authors rarely do that.
same problem with Atom (difference in hAtom)
Yeah, it does.
A bigger question: what constitutes an ISO 8601 date?
IIRC all three forms require an exact day
So 2008-11 wouldn't be a date
http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_8601
&quot;time point representing a calendar day on a time scale consisting of an origin and a succession of calendar days&quot;
That's a date according to ISO 8601:2004 section 2.1.8
btw - mentioning &quot;parsers have to cope with...&quot; implies an underlying assertion of optimizing for parsers, and propagating that value assumption is perhaps as bad as making it oneself, perhaps worse. Try to train yourself to not bother even mentioning it as a side-effect, otherwise you will imply it as an objective.
I tend not to be so clear on IRC (or in any sync form of communication)

