conv_id=135720 | prompt="
CONVERSATION:
ab7faae5d7b250ea8606486575f8f79c: would it be possible to list the top values of attributes? like http://canvex.lazyilluminati.com/misc/stats/2/analyse.cgi/tagattr/a/target
73eaf221abca..." | raw="
CONVERSATION:
ab7faae5d7b250ea8606486575f8f79c: would it be possible to list the top values of attributes? like http://canvex.lazyilluminati.com/misc/stats/2/analyse.cgi/tagattr/a/target
73eaf221abca1b8244e34b0b9e76ac46: I don't think I can really do that in general, since I'd have to store every attribute value in order to count them at the end, and attribute values make up about a third of all the bytes in pages, so that's too much data to store easily
(or am I missing cleverer ways to count attribute value frequency?)
But I could do it much more easily if there's just a small set of attributes that are worth counting
ok
uses of &lt;noscript&gt;: ads (document.write+&lt;noscript&gt;), tracking (script + &lt;noscript&gt;&lt;img&gt;), inclusion of flash (plugin detection with script + &lt;noscript&gt;&lt;object&gt;)
from analysing the first 10 pages in http://canvex.lazyilluminati.com/misc/stats/2/analyse.cgi/tag/noscript
the first doesn't work at all in xml. the second neither, since &lt;img&gt;s are always loaded (except in opera). the third might work
Maybe I shouldn't sort the list of pages by the number of occurrences? The first ones in the list may not be very representative, since they're using the feature an abnormal number of times
indeed
from the bottom 10 pages: normal content (document.write+&lt;noscript&gt;), tracking, inclusion of css (&lt;noscript&gt;&lt;style&gt;), ads, inclusion of navigation (document.write+&lt;noscript&gt;&lt;table&gt;)
73eaf221abca1b8244e34b0b9e76ac46: http://canvex.lazyilluminati.com/misc/stats/2/analyse.cgi/pages/tag/noscript
...should provide a more uniform selection of pages
It's a bit odd how I see different element distribution compared to http://triin.net/2006/06/12/HTML, when looking at the same set of pages
since I think a sample of ~8K pages ought to be something like +/- 1% of the true mean, but there are bigger variations than that
though that one was a year ago, so maybe that's the reason for the changes
It's slightly irritating that I can't find the actual numbers used to generate the graphs like http://triin.net/archive/kool/webstat/figure-13.png, but it looks like there's a notable increase in meta, link, script, span, div
and a notable decrease in table, tr, td, font, b, center
(By &quot;notable&quot; I mean something around 2-3%)
The XHTML 1.0 Transitional doctype has gone up from ~5% to ~11%
If things have changed that much in a year, maybe the web isn't actually a lost cause
looking through the first few in that list, i find an empty &lt;noscript&gt; and a &lt;noscript&gt;&lt;meta refresh&gt;
where the meta refresh goes to the same page but with a query string
of the 30-40 pages i've looked at now, i haven't found one single saying &quot;Seems like your browser doesn't support JS!&quot; or anything similar
but perhaps that's more common on web apps
http://www.google.com/search?q=you-have-javascript-disabled
Eek - 5% of dmoz.org's data is CNN.com
At least it looks like they don't change their old pages when they redesign the site, so they wouldn't single-handedly cause a 5% shift in all the measured statistics...

