3f21e54c5c5ffd50fe3680acc025bb1d: is redefining current behavior frowned upon in general?
i'd like to tweak the meaning of italic
to mean &quot;use the italic face with no artificial sloping&quot;
and add &quot;sloped&quot;
as a way to indicate
befddef7b56a6caf71fa21f2235a8111: redefining existing behavior is discouraged for backwards-compatibility reasons
hmm, pooh
befddef7b56a6caf71fa21f2235a8111: italic already means &quot;italic face with no artificial sloping&quot;
heh
befddef7b56a6caf71fa21f2235a8111: it just allows falling back to artificially-sloped fonts
befddef7b56a6caf71fa21f2235a8111: I think that's important to keep
ah, ok, i'll buy that
befddef7b56a6caf71fa21f2235a8111: what about 'oblique'? how is that different from 'sloped'?
oblique is a designers stylistic choice
it's also a value for font-style
it reflects a font face that is not cursive in nature
i'm looking for a way to allow &quot;no artificial sloped fonts&quot;
to be specified
no-faux?
font-style: true italic?
yeah, something like that
syntax ugly though...
that would change font family if there is no italic available?
would use regular face
hm
what would you use it for?
cases where designer prefers no italics to fake italics
so font-style: italic-or-normal? :)
basically
yeah, you'd need a new keyword
it's important to keep the existing behavior here: one major use of italic is to distinguish certain text runs from other text runs
and you totally lose that if we get snobby about italics
for heading styles, yeah, I can see wanting italic-or-normal
but I think that's not the major case, and someone should be very aware that what they're doing can result in a normal, rather than italic, font being used
i.e. the fallback behavior here needs to be blazingly obvious from the syntax
yeah
fallback makes this completely non-obvious
note that downloadable fonts would make this much less useful
since if you care that much about font particulars that you'll accept a normal face in place of italic, you probably care enough to send along a copy of the font you want :)
well, not completely true
there are also language cases where you want italic instances to use the regular face
like arabic
Example: Arial has arabic glyphs, Arial Italic does not
so how the fallback occurs is not readily clear
ah, yes
that's an interesting case
you probably just want to ignore the italic setting
or slant left instead of right
but that's getting a bit fancy
there's no italic vs. regular equivalent in arabic from my understanding
I'd check with Paul Nelson
He's got a lot of experience with international typography
yeah, i saw that he did the OT table  work for the Arabic Typography font that MS produced
He's taking a break from the CSSWG because he's got too many other projects, but he'll be happy to answer questions.
Steve Zilles is also a good person to talk with.
And of course you can always shoot questions at the i18n working group on www-international. :)
But I'd definitely hook Paul into discussions about things like this
He's even in your timezone, almost ;)
will do, although he seems kinda busy when i contacted him before

