69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: former in Firefox, latter in IE
well clearly imho aaabbb element is outright incorrect.
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: you want to spec what IE does?
WebKit is element aaabbb, IIRC
and Opera
the spec already requires the text after the &lt;script&gt; to come after whatever state the DOM is in after the script executes.
element aaa bbb is definitely wrong per spec, it means the parser isn't re-entrant.
(or acts as if it's not, which is worse)
http://www.hixie.ch/tests/adhoc/html/parsing/026-demo.html
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: the webkit/opera behavior flows naturally from a late-flushed accumulation buffer
webkit's behaviour is clearly wrong.
then a late-flushed accumulation buffer is a bad dea.
idea
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: it's 2 out of 4 :-)
I'll have to get back on this when I have a decent text input method
IE's behaviour in http://www.hixie.ch/tests/adhoc/html/parsing/026-demo.html is what the spec requires
and is the only behaviour i've found in the four browsers i tested that is even remotely sane.
anyway, as i noted in my e-mail reply, just use the text node's own buffer as the buffer
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: you have an interesting notion of sane
and then you get IE's behaviour for free
really? you don't think having things come out in the order they were written is sane?
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: nope, then you get the Firefox behavior
